\subsection{Computer-Assisted Analytical Closure of the Live Certificate}
\label{sec:iss-computer-assisted-certificate}

The finite-horizon certificate of Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-live-certificate}
separates the logical stability implication from the numerical task of
constructing uniform constants.  The eight-sea diagnostic demonstrates that
the deployed estimator contracts strongly in its realized Riccati metric, but
it does not provide a uniform theorem.  This subsection closes the linear
finite-horizon implication by a verified-arithmetic calculation over a declared
certificate domain.  The calculation is analytical in the sense relevant here:
it evaluates closed-form inequalities over intervals and contains no simulated
sea, random seed, sampled wave spectrum, or fitted stability margin.

\subsubsection{Certificate domain and source contract}

The proof tool is
\texttt{tools/ou\_live\_basin\_interval\_proof.py}.  It first checks the source
tree for the proof-relevant default constants and aborts if they drift.  The
source/default part of the domain uses the default tuning path
$\tau_\star=(2f_{\rm tune})^{-1}$ with $f_{\rm tune}\le\SI{1.5}{Hz}$ and the
source ceiling $\tau\le\SI{12}{s}$, hence
\begin{equation}
  \frac13\ {\rm s}\le\tau\le12\ {\rm s}.
  \label{eq:iss-ca-tau-domain}
\end{equation}
It also uses $\sigma_{aw}\le\SI{6}{m/s^2}$, the default
$\tau_b=\SI{5000}{s}$ residual-bias model, the validated sensor-noise
configuration, and the effective $S$-pseudo standard-deviation enclosure
\begin{equation}
  0.044\le r_S\le694\ {\rm m\,s}.
  \label{eq:iss-ca-rs-domain}
\end{equation}
The latter is the conservative post-cadence enclosure already pinned by the
implementation contract test; it is deliberately wider than the base
$[0.15,400]$ clamp.

Not every theorem hypothesis is enforced by the current source.  The verified
calculation therefore labels the remaining quantities as
\emph{certificate-domain assumptions}, rather than implementation guarantees:
\begin{equation}
\begin{gathered}
\SI{4.5}{ms}\le h_k\le\SI{5.5}{ms},\qquad
\sigma_{aw}\ge\SI{0.01}{m/s^2},\\
5\le\|\widehat f_b\|\le15\ {\rm m/s^2},\qquad
10\le\|\widehat m_b\|\le100\ {\rm \mu T},\\
1-|u_f^\top u_m|\ge0.05,\qquad
\|\widehat\omega\|\le\SI{1.5}{rad/s},\\
\SI{5}{ms}\le\Delta\le\SI{50}{ms}
\end{gathered}
\label{eq:iss-ca-external-domain}
\end{equation}
for the selected accepted vector-update pair.  In particular, the production
tuner has no positive lower clamp on $\sigma_{aw}$.  Equation
\eqref{eq:iss-ca-external-domain} is therefore a condition under which an
interval may be called certified; it is not silently attributed to the current
implementation.

\subsubsection{Exact finite-horizon covariance decomposition}

Consider the linearized, scaled error system over one proof horizon,
\begin{equation}
 z_{i+1}=F_i z_i+B_iw_i,\qquad
 y_i=C_i z_i+v_i ,
\end{equation}
with the same accepted-update schedule as the Live theorem.  Stack the
independent process noises after whitening into $\xi$.  At the horizon endpoint
one may write
\begin{equation}
 z_H=\Phi_H z_0+\mathcal B_H\xi ,
 \qquad
 Y=\mathcal O_Hz_0+\mathcal H_H\xi+v .
 \label{eq:iss-ca-batch}
\end{equation}
For the actual Kalman estimator, linearity gives the covariance decomposition
\begin{equation}
 P_H=\Psi_HP_0\Psi_H^\top+\Omega_H ,
 \label{eq:iss-ca-cov-decomp}
\end{equation}
where $\Psi_H$ is the homogeneous closed-loop transition and $\Omega_H$ is the
error covariance generated by process and measurement noise with zero initial
error.

Give an auxiliary estimator the true initial state $z_0$.  Conditional on that
additional information, estimating the process-noise contribution
$\mathcal B_H\xi$ from
$\mathcal H_H\xi+v$ is a linear Gaussian problem.  Its minimum covariance is
\begin{equation}
 P_{\rm or}
 =
 \mathcal B_H
 \left(I+\mathcal H_H^\top R^{-1}\mathcal H_H\right)^{-1}
 \mathcal B_H^\top .
 \label{eq:iss-ca-oracle}
\end{equation}
The noise-only part of the actual Kalman estimator is another linear estimator
of the same quantity but without the oracle information; consequently
\begin{equation}
 \Omega_H\succeq P_{\rm or}.
 \label{eq:iss-ca-oracle-order}
\end{equation}
If
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal B_H\mathcal B_H^\top\succeq\alpha_c I,
 \qquad
 \|\mathcal H_H^\top R^{-1}\mathcal H_H\|_2\le\beta_c ,
 \label{eq:iss-ca-alpha-beta}
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\Omega_H\succeq q_H I,\qquad
 q_H:=\frac{\alpha_c}{1+\beta_c}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-qh}
\end{equation}

Suppose independently that the explicit finite-window observer constructed
below yields
\begin{equation}
 P_H\preceq\overline p I .
 \label{eq:iss-ca-pbar}
\end{equation}
Substitution of Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-qh}--\eqref{eq:iss-ca-pbar} into
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-cov-decomp} gives
\begin{equation}
 \Psi_HP_0\Psi_H^\top
 \preceq
 \left(1-\delta_H\right)P_H,\qquad
 \delta_H:=\frac{q_H}{\overline p}>0 .
 \label{eq:iss-ca-delta}
\end{equation}
Hence, if $P_i=L_iL_i^\top$,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|L_H^{-1}\Psi_HL_0\|_2
 \le \sqrt{1-\delta_H}<1 .}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-contraction}
\end{equation}
This establishes strict finite-horizon Riccati-metric contraction without
evaluating $\Psi_H$ on a simulated trajectory.

\subsubsection{Uniform process floor for an arbitrarily varying schedule}

The computer-assisted proof does not freeze $\tau$ in the theorem used for
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-contraction}.  For one OU--III axis and one IMU sample,
write $x=h/\tau$ and factor the scaled exact process covariance as
\begin{equation}
 Q_{L,z}=q_ch D_h\widehat W(x)D_h,\qquad
 q_c=\frac{2\sigma_{aw}^2}{\tau},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
 D_h=\operatorname{diag}
 \left(\frac{h}{s_v},\frac{h^2}{s_p},
       \frac{h^3}{s_S},\frac{1}{s_{aw}}\right).
\end{equation}
The four dimensionless impulse functions satisfy the elementary bounds used in
the script, which imply
\begin{equation}
 \det\widehat W(x)
 \ge
 \frac{e^{-2x}}{870912000},
 \qquad
 \operatorname{tr}\widehat W(x)\le\frac{437}{315}.
\end{equation}
Therefore
\begin{equation}
 \lambda_{\min}(\widehat W)
 \ge
 \frac{e^{-2x}}
 {870912000(437/315)^3}.
\end{equation}
Using the endpoint values of the declared intervals gives a one-sample,
schedule-independent lower bound
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 q_{\rm step}\ge1.036520021409\times10^{-34}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-qstep-num}
\end{equation}
Because the final prediction in a proof horizon contributes this positive
covariance, $\alpha_c=q_{\rm step}$ is sufficient even when $\tau$ changes
arbitrarily from sample to sample inside the certificate domain.

For comparison only, if $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ are held fixed over the
complete maximum proof horizon
$T_H=\SI{0.5074090909}{s}$, applying the same analytical bound with
$h=T_H$ gives
\begin{equation}
 q_{\rm frozen}\ge1.182332737117\times10^{-21},
 \label{eq:iss-ca-frozen-q}
\end{equation}
about $1.14\times10^{13}$ larger than
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-qstep-num}.  Equation
\eqref{eq:iss-ca-frozen-q} is a diagnostic strengthening, not a hypothesis
inserted into the time-varying theorem.

\subsubsection{Constructive covariance upper bound}

The four bounded-gap $S$ observations recover the scalar
$[v,p,S,a_w]$ state by divided differences.  The third divided difference
annihilates the quadratic $S_0+t p_0+t^2v_0/2$ part and yields $a_w$.  With
successive gaps no smaller than $T_-$, its weight vector obeys
\begin{equation}
 \|w_3\|_2\le\frac{\sqrt5}{3T_-^3},
\end{equation}
while the Hermite--Genocchi bound already used in
Lemma~\ref{lem:iss-axis-uco} gives
\begin{equation}
 A_3[t_0,t_1,t_2,t_3]
 \ge\frac16e^{-3T_+/\underline\tau}.
\end{equation}
After $a_w$ is removed, second and first divided differences recover $v$ and
$p$, and $S$ is the $t_0=0$ sample.  Carrying the fixed state scaling and the
global $r_S$ enclosure through this explicit inverse gives the verified
one-axis bound
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal O_L)
 \ge1.216353822821\times10^{-15}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-sl}
\end{equation}

The two-vector attitude/gyro-bias construction gives, under
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-external-domain},
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal O_A)
 \ge3.161536500037\times10^{-5}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-sa}
\end{equation}
Combining the translational and attitude blocks through the actual
block-triangular observation structure and bounding the acceleration-to-vector
cross block yields
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal O_{18})
 \ge6.707390504261\times10^{-23}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-s18}
\end{equation}

A finite-window least-squares observer for these 18 coordinates, with the
stable residual accelerometer bias treated as bounded colored nuisance, is an
admissible estimator using the same measurements as the Kalman filter.  Its
explicit covariance therefore upper-bounds the optimal Kalman covariance.
The calculation uses
\begin{equation}
 \overline P_{b_a}
 =
 \max\left\{
 (0.004/0.5)^2,\,
 \frac{(2.5\times10^{-7})5000}{2(0.5)^2}
 \right\}
 =2.5\times10^{-3}
\end{equation}
in scaled coordinates, together with closed-form transition and accumulated
process-noise envelopes.  The resulting verified full-state bound is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\overline p
 \le4.176061086161\times10^{57}.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-pbar-num}
\end{equation}
The number is intentionally not presented as a realistic covariance estimate:
it is a worst-case comparison-estimator bound over the complete declared box.

\subsubsection{Verified result and interpretation}

The process-to-selected-measurement operator is bounded by
\begin{equation}
 \beta_c\le1.052749938580\times10^{10},
\end{equation}
so the oracle argument yields
\begin{equation}
 q_H\ge9.845833120718\times10^{-45}.
\end{equation}
Together with Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-pbar-num},
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \delta_H\ge2.357684171179\times10^{-102}>0.}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-delta-num}
\end{equation}
Consequently
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 1-\chi_H
 \ge1.178842085590\times10^{-102}>0,}
 \label{eq:iss-ca-chi-margin}
\end{equation}
where the last inequality uses
$1-\sqrt{1-\delta}\ge\delta/2$.

This is a constructive proof of strict contraction on the declared
certificate domain; it is not a useful numerical handoff radius.  The
calculation identifies two dominant losses.  First, insisting on arbitrary
sample-to-sample scheduling forces the universal process floor to inherit the
triple-integrator one-sample scale.  Freezing the schedule over the same horizon
raises the corresponding verified $\delta$ lower bound to
\begin{equation}
 2.689351987219\times10^{-89},
\end{equation}
approximately thirteen orders of magnitude, but remains far from the realized
Riccati contraction.  Second, and more importantly, the covariance upper bound
is dominated by the global four-$S$-measurement inverse and the
block-triangular cross coupling, producing the very small
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-s18}.  Thus the broad-box comparison estimator, not an
observed instability of the filter, is the principal obstruction to a useful
deterministic basin constant.

The practical conclusion is deliberately narrow.  The computer-assisted
calculation closes the previously existential linear UES implication with
strict, reproducible constants and proves that no reference-sea simulation is
needed for that implication.  It does \emph{not} justify replacing the
Phase-2 measured $\chi_{V,30}\approx0.94$ by
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-chi-margin} for engineering tuning, and it does not yet
supply a practically sized nonlinear handoff tube.  A useful theorem-certified
runtime basin requires a sharper covariance comparison---most naturally one
that exploits the coupled deployed $r_S(\tau,\sigma_{aw})$ schedule and a
verified slew bound rather than combining mutually incompatible global
worst cases.  No such coupling is assumed in the result above.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.}
The proof script uses Python \texttt{Decimal} arithmetic at high precision.
Algebraic operations are rounded in the adverse direction; correctly rounded
transcendentals are widened by one ulp before they enter a certified bound.
The program checks the source/default constants, emits all intermediate
constants, and exits unsuccessfully unless
$q_H>0$, $\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal O_{18})>0$, and
$\delta_H>0$.  Its frozen-schedule comparison is printed separately and is not
used to pass the uniform theorem.
